// 1. What st.size() actually returns
// When you call st.size(), you might expect it to return a standard int. However, it actually returns a type called size_t (specifically std::stack::size_type).

// size_t is an unsigned integer.

// C++ containers use unsigned integers for their sizes because a container can never have a negative number of elements. Using an unsigned type allows the container to theoretically hold twice as many elements as a standard signed int.

// 2. The std::max Type Mismatch
// In C++, std::max is a template function. This means the compiler figures out what type of data it is comparing by looking at the arguments you pass in.

// However, std::max has a strict rule: both arguments must be of the exact same type.

// C++
// int max_depth = 0; // This is a signed integer
// st.size();         // This is an unsigned integer (size_t)

// // The compiler gets confused here:
// max_depth = max(max_depth, st.size()); 
// When the compiler looks at max(max_depth, st.size()), it sees one int and one size_t. It doesnt know whether it should compare them as two signed integers or two unsigned integers, so it throws a compilation error: “no matching function for call to max”.

// 3. The Fix
// By casting st.size() to an int using (int)st.size(), you are forcing both arguments to be standard signed integers. The compiler is happy, and std::max works perfectly.

// Alternative Solutions
// Instead of typecasting, you could also solve this in two other ways:

// Alternative A: Change the type of max_depth
// You can declare max_depth as a size_t from the beginning. Both variables will be unsigned integers.

// C++
// size_t max_depth = 0;
// // ...
// max_depth = max(max_depth, st.size()); // No cast needed
// return (int)max_depth; // Cast at the very end to match the function return type
// Alternative B: Explicitly tell std::max what type to use
// You can use angle brackets <int> to explicitly tell the template to treat everything as an int.

// C++
// int max_depth = 0;
// // ...
// max_depth = max<int>(max_depth, st.size());

// TC: O(n) where n is the length of string. We iterate through the string exactly once. Pushing and popping from the stack takes O(1) time.

// SC: O(n) in worst case scenario. If the string consists entirely of opening parentheses (eg: ((((( ) the stack will store all of them requiring space proportional to the length of string.

// 2. Optimal Solution: 
// Since the problem guarantees that the string is a VPS we dont actually need to store the characters in a stack. We just need to keep track of how many unmatched opening parentheses we currently have.

// We can replace the stack with a simple integer counter. We increment the counter for ( and decrement it for )

class Solution {
public:
    int maxDepth(string s) {
        int current_depth = 0;
        int max_depth = 0;

        for (char c : s) {
            if (c == '(') {
                current_depth++;
                max_depth = max(max_depth, current_depth);
            } else if (c == ')') {
                current_depth--;
            }
        }

        return max_depth;
    }
};

